```


Please ensure you have installed the Pex & Reflector SDKs and enabled the contract testing at your IDE setting for this to work. Also, it's important to have tested your production code with Pex to find any regression bugs before applying this testing methodology.
Do not forget to add using statements for the necessary libraries. In my case, I needed to add the using statement "using System.Diagnostics;" for Debug.Assert to work.
Also, the contract testing could be done only on methods (at least in c#) that change the state of the system under test, like removing or inserting elements in a list. Hence, ensure you choose the correct method to apply the contract testing. 

In my example, I have shown contracts by modifying List methods (like Add, Remove etc.). You can show contracts for other methods as per your needs.

Make sure to carefully annotate the assertions and tests and explain the purpose and the expected behavior. It will help others understand your code and tests better.

Please note that the Pex tool is very much a tool for automatic generation of tests and does not replace a critical analysis of the code by a human tester. So, always cross check your tests and contracts and adjust them accordingly when needed. In addition, writing a lot of tests may increase the overall maintenance cost. So, choose a balance in applying Pex to your projects.
Hope this helps someone trying to apply Pex to a project. If you have any other queries or need further assistance, feel free to ask :)﻿using System;
